      "text": "To determine the probability that the two-digit number \\(ab\\) (where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the outcomes of two fair 6-sided dice) and the digits \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are all divisible by 3, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the possible outcomes for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n2. Determine which of these outcomes are divisible by 3.\n3. Calculate the total number of favorable outcomes.\n4. Compute the probability.\n\nFirst, note that each die has 6 faces, so there are \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\) possible outcomes when two dice are tossed. The digits \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must be chosen from the set \\(\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\}\\), but only those whose sum is divisible by 3 will be considered favorable.\n\nWe list the sums that are divisible by 3 within the range of possible sums (which is \\(2\\) to \\(12\\)):\n\n- \\(1 + 2 = 3\\)\n- \\(1 + 5 = 6\\)\n- \\(2 + 3 = 5\\)\n- \\(2 + 6 = 8\\)\n- \\(3 + 4 = 7\\)\n- \\(3 + 5 = 8\\)\n- \\(4 + 6 = 10\\)\n- \\(5 + 6 = 11\\)\n- \\(6 + 6 = 12\\)\n\nHere, the sums 3, 6, 12 are divisible by 3, and the sums 1, 4, 5, 8, 7, 10, 11 are not. Including the trivial case of \\(0 + 0 = 0\\) (which is divisible by 3) but not counting it as a two-digit number \\(ab\\), we have 9 favorable sums.\n\nNext, we count the number of ways to achieve these sums:\n- \\(3 \\rightarrow (1, 2), (2, 1)\\) — 2 ways\n- \\(6 \\rightarrow (1, 5), (2, 4), (3, 3), (4, 2), (5, 1)\\) — 5 ways\n\nAdding these, we get \\(2 + 5 = 7\\) favorable outcomes. Each of the 24 ways to roll a sum of 9, 15, or 16 (i.e., 410, 453, 354, 591) can also be satisfied with one of the numbers being 0, making 410 = 12, 1116 = 62, 2431 = 29, 5491 = 50. However, 0 is not 3 or 6, so we check 913 and 614: 129, 1591.93154, 3691, 5691, making \\(0 = 12\\), 410 = 59): Therefore no extraneous favorable outcomes. Now we sum the 40 pairs. Now shows probability function to get Figure:\n  Finally, we calculate the probability:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{12}{36} = \\frac{1}{3}\n  \\]\nThus, the probability is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]",
